# /data is never mounted, so post-fs-data fails every mkdir with "Read-only file
# system" (it is writing into the read-only rootfs) and "vdc cryptfs init_user0"
# then fails, which makes init reboot into recovery with
# --prompt_and_wipe_data --reason=init_user0_failed.
#
# Nothing in AOSP mounts /data: init.rc only triggers late-fs, and the vendor is
# expected to call mount_all --late from it. The only such call on this device is
# in /vendor/etc/init/hw/init.target.rc:
#
#   on late-fs
#       wait_for_prop hwservicemanager.ready true
#       mount_all --late
#
# In two independent ramoops captures that action never executed: no "processing
# action (late-fs) from (.../init.target.rc:87)", no "start_waiting_for_property"
# (which wait_for_prop logs when the property is already set), no mount_all, and
# no F2FS-fs mount message anywhere. The file itself is intact - 177 lines, md5
# matches the stock blob, no CR, correct syntax - and its earlier sections
# (early-init, init, early-fs, fs, post-fs) all run. Why that one action is
# skipped is not understood.
#
# Rather than depend on it, mount /data from here. Actions in /system/etc/init
# are known to run on this device: init.cypfr-bootprobe.rc fires at init, fs and
# post-fs in the same captures. Init parses /system/etc/init after the vendor
# imports, so if the vendor action does run after all, it runs first and this is
# a no-op - mount_all skips entries that are already mounted.
#
# The kmsg markers are deliberate: the ramoops console is the only log available
# this early, and about 5% of its bytes come back corrupted, so a distinctive
# string is far easier to find than an absent one is to prove missing.

on late-fs
    write /dev/kmsg "cypfr: late-fs reached, calling mount_all --late"
    mount_all --late
    write /dev/kmsg "cypfr: mount_all --late returned"
    # DIAGNOSTIC, remove once /data mounts. The ramoops console is unreliable
    # here - about 5% of its bytes come back corrupted and after some reboots it
    # comes back empty - but /metadata is mounted in first stage and can be read
    # from recovery, so dump the kernel log there while we still can. This has to
    # happen in late-fs: init.rc's own post-fs-data action reboots at init_user0
    # before any of ours would run.
    exec -- /system/bin/sh -c "/system/bin/dmesg > /metadata/cypfr-bootlog.txt; /system/bin/logcat -d -b all > /metadata/cypfr-logcat.txt; sync"

on post-fs-data
    write /dev/kmsg "cypfr: post-fs-data reached"
